 ## Overview
-This family generates voxel data by leveraging graphics hardware pipelines, specifically rasterization and geometry shaders, to map triangle meshes into a 3D grid [S1, unofficial]. It differs from geometric clipping methods by utilizing fragment or stream output to determine voxel occupancy, offering high performance for real-time applications at the cost of potential gaps if conservative rasterization is not used [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
+This family converts triangle meshes into discrete volumetric representations (voxels) by leveraging GPU hardware rasterization pipelines or programmable shaders, rather than CPU-based geometric intersection tests. It solves the problem of real-time or high-throughput voxelization for volume rendering and fluid simulation by mapping 2D rasterization concepts or explicit geometry generation to 3D grid occupancy [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [Voxel Carving](1213-voxel-carving.md) or [Implicit Surface Voxelization](2379-implicit-surface-voxelization.md) by relying on the graphics pipeline's fragment processing or geometry shaders to determine voxel inclusion, often producing explicit cube meshes or point clouds rather than dense occupancy grids [S1, S4, S3, unofficial].
 
